Say what time it is and whether a match is running.

The frame note has carried the geometry and the middle pixel since the port
first drew anything, and both questions it was written for are answered: the
frame is called, and something is in the buffer. What it cannot say is either of
the two things the port is now being asked:

* **How fast.** The note fires every three hundredth frame and prints no clock,
  so the rate had to be divided out of the wall time of the whole run -- and only
  for a run that ended exactly when the screenshots did. The menu turns out to
  draw ten frames a second and the mod-selection screen thirty-six, which is a
  factor the note never showed.
* **Whether a match is running at all.** "The menu draws" and "it plays" are
  different claims. The second one is the one that exercises the simulation,
  Petra, the terrain and the unit meshes -- and the only one that touches the
  JIT-less SpiderMonkey with real work.

So: the clock goes into the line, and with it the turn number, behind the guard
Frame() uses (the turn manager exists only while a game does). And the note also
fires every ten seconds, not only every three hundredth frame: a match in the
simulator's software renderer is expected around a frame a second, where 300
frames would be five minutes of timeline with nothing in it.

--- source/main.cpp
+++ source/main.cpp
@@ -661,8 +661,16 @@
 void IosFrameCallback(void*)
 {
 	static int frames{0};
-	if (frames < 3 || frames % 300 == 0)
+	static Uint32 lastNote{0};
+	// Every ten seconds as well as every three hundredth frame. A match in the
+	// simulator's software renderer is expected at about a frame a second, and
+	// 300 frames are then five minutes with nothing in between; the menu draws
+	// ten times that and the mod-selection screen thirty-six times. Only a
+	// clock in the line makes any of them comparable.
+	const Uint32 now{SDL_GetTicks()};
+	if (frames < 3 || frames % 300 == 0 || now - lastNote >= 10000)
 	{
+		lastNote = now;
 		// What the engine believes it is drawing into, and what is actually in
 		// the middle of the buffer when the frame is done. A screenshot of the
 		// simulator can be misleading -- this cannot.
@@ -673,12 +681,21 @@
 		unsigned char pixel[4]{};
 		glReadPixels(drawableWidth / 2, drawableHeight / 2, 1, 1,
 			GL_RGBA, GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE, pixel);
+		// Whether a match is running, and which turn it is on: "the menu draws"
+		// and "it plays" are different claims, and only the second one puts the
+		// simulation, Petra and the terrain to work. The guard is the one
+		// Frame() uses -- the turn manager exists only while a game does.
+		char match[32]{"no match"};
+		if (g_Game && g_Game->IsGameStarted() && g_Game->GetTurnManager())
+			snprintf(match, sizeof(match), "turn %u",
+				g_Game->GetTurnManager()->GetCurrentTurn());
 		// g_xres/g_yres as well: the viewport comes from those, the backbuffer
 		// from the drawable, and when the two disagree the game draws into a
 		// corner of its own frame. Nothing else prints them.
-		IosNote("frame %d: window %dx%d, drawable %dx%d, viewport %dx%d, middle pixel %u,%u,%u",
-			frames, windowWidth, windowHeight, drawableWidth, drawableHeight,
-			g_xres, g_yres, pixel[0], pixel[1], pixel[2]);
+		IosNote("frame %d at %u ms: window %dx%d, drawable %dx%d, viewport %dx%d, "
+			"middle pixel %u,%u,%u, %s",
+			frames, now, windowWidth, windowHeight, drawableWidth, drawableHeight,
+			g_xres, g_yres, pixel[0], pixel[1], pixel[2], match);
 	}
 	++frames;
 
